      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "tutorial-about-twilite"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## How to read Twilite\n\n1. Ask whether this graph is teaching a concept, guiding a workflow, or acting as a real application surface.\n2. Look for the declaration, exposed surfaces, and navigation model.\n3. Treat nodes as authored meaning, not just boxes on a canvas.\n4. Expect some parts of the ontology to feel more complete than the current public runtime.\n5. Read the tutorials as both instruction and evidence of the model.\n\nTwilite usually makes more sense when you ask what role a graph is playing."
        }
      }
    },
